# PML-005 — Change Management Standard

**Project:** Trading Intelligence Platform (TIP)
**Document ID:** PML-005
**Version:** 1.0.0
**Status:** Approved (Initial Baseline)
**Classification:** Project Governing Standard
**Owner:** TIP Architecture & Engineering Team
**Effective Date:** 2026-08-08

---

# 1. Purpose

The Change Management Standard defines the controlled process for proposing, evaluating, approving, implementing, verifying, and deploying changes to the Trading Intelligence Platform (TIP).

The objective is to ensure that every change improves the platform while protecting architectural integrity, software quality, security, operational stability, and traceability.

No change shall be implemented outside the processes defined in this standard.

---

# 2. Scope

This standard applies to all changes affecting:

* Business requirements
* Functional requirements
* Enterprise architecture
* Project Master Library (PML)
* Architecture Decision Records (ADR)
* Source code
* Database schema
* APIs
* Infrastructure
* Test suites
* CI/CD pipelines
* Deployment scripts
* Production environments
* Documentation

---

# 3. Change Management Principles

Every change shall be:

* Business justified.
* Technically reviewed.
* Risk assessed.
* Fully traceable.
* Tested before release.
* Properly documented.
* Approved by the appropriate authority.
* Reversible where practical.

---

# 4. Change Categories

| Category         | Description                                                             |
| ---------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Standard Change  | Low-risk, pre-approved recurring change                                 |
| Normal Change    | Planned change requiring review and approval                            |
| Major Change     | High-impact architectural or business change                            |
| Emergency Change | Urgent production change to restore service or resolve a critical issue |

The change category determines the required level of governance.

---

# 5. Change Request Process

Every proposed change shall follow the lifecycle below.

```text id="gmr3xp"
Identify Need
      │
      ▼
Create Change Request
      │
      ▼
Impact Assessment
      │
      ▼
Architecture Review
      │
      ▼
Risk Assessment
      │
      ▼
Approval
      │
      ▼
Implementation
      │
      ▼
Testing
      │
      ▼
Documentation Update
      │
      ▼
Release
      │
      ▼
Post-Implementation Review
      │
      ▼
Close Change
```

---

# 6. Change Request Contents

Every Change Request (CR) shall include:

* Change ID
* Title
* Description
* Business justification
* Scope
* Impact assessment
* Risks
* Dependencies
* Estimated effort
* Proposed implementation date
* Rollback strategy
* Approval status

Example:

```text id="j5k5jt"
CR-2026-001

Implement Market Structure Engine v1.0
```

---

# 7. Impact Assessment

Every change shall assess the impact on:

* Business functionality
* Architecture
* Source code
* Database
* APIs
* Security
* Performance
* Documentation
* Testing
* Operations

Where significant architectural impact exists, a new ADR shall be created or an existing ADR updated.

---

# 8. Risk Assessment

Changes shall be classified by risk.

| Risk Level | Description                                                                |
| ---------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Low        | Minimal impact                                                             |
| Medium     | Moderate impact with manageable risk                                       |
| High       | Significant technical or operational impact                                |
| Critical   | Potential impact on production stability, security, or business continuity |

Higher-risk changes require additional review and testing.

---

# 9. Approval Authority

| Change Type | Approval Authority                                           |
| ----------- | ------------------------------------------------------------ |
| Standard    | Technical Lead                                               |
| Normal      | Technical Lead + Solution Architect                          |
| Major       | Product Owner + Solution Architect                           |
| Emergency   | Technical Lead (immediate), followed by retrospective review |

No implementation shall begin before the required approvals have been obtained, except in authorised emergency situations.

---

# 10. Implementation Standards

Approved changes shall:

* Follow applicable PML standards.
* Be developed on an appropriate Git branch.
* Include automated tests where applicable.
* Include documentation updates.
* Preserve backward compatibility unless otherwise approved.

---

# 11. Testing Requirements

Before release, changes shall complete:

* Unit testing.
* Integration testing.
* Regression testing.
* Security testing (where applicable).
* Performance testing (where applicable).
* User acceptance testing (when required).

Testing evidence shall be retained with the change record.

---

# 12. Documentation Requirements

Every approved change shall update affected documentation, including:

* PML documents
* ADRs
* Architecture documents
* Technical design
* API documentation
* Database documentation
* User documentation
* Release notes

Documentation updates are mandatory before the change is considered complete.

---

# 13. Deployment

Changes shall be deployed using the approved CI/CD pipeline.

Each deployment shall include:

* Version number
* Build identifier
* Commit reference
* Deployment record
* Rollback instructions
* Validation results

Manual production deployments shall be avoided wherever practical.

---

# 14. Emergency Changes

Emergency changes are permitted only to:

* Restore production service.
* Address critical security vulnerabilities.
* Resolve severe production defects.

Emergency changes shall:

1. Be documented immediately.
2. Undergo retrospective review.
3. Be incorporated into the standard development baseline after validation.

---

# 15. Post-Implementation Review

Following implementation, the project shall verify:

* Objectives achieved.
* No unintended impacts.
* Documentation updated.
* Tests completed.
* Monitoring confirms stable operation.
* Lessons learned captured.

Significant findings shall be fed back into the continuous improvement process.

---

# 16. Audit & Traceability

Every change shall be traceable to:

* Business requirements
* Functional requirements
* PML standards
* ADRs
* Source code commits
* Test evidence
* Release notes

Git history forms part of the official audit record.

---

# 17. Roles & Responsibilities

| Role               | Responsibilities                  |
| ------------------ | --------------------------------- |
| Product Owner      | Approves business scope changes   |
| Solution Architect | Reviews architectural impact      |
| Technical Lead     | Oversees technical implementation |
| Developers         | Implement approved changes        |
| QA Engineers       | Verify quality and testing        |
| DevOps Engineer    | Manage deployments                |
| Operations         | Validate production readiness     |

---

# 18. Compliance

Compliance with this standard is mandatory.

Changes that bypass the defined process shall not become part of the official Trading Intelligence Platform baseline unless formally reviewed and approved.

---

# 19. Change Management Statement

The Change Management Standard ensures that every modification to the Trading Intelligence Platform is governed, traceable, risk-assessed, tested, documented, and aligned with the long-term architectural vision of the project.

